`requestMatchers()` with `HttpMethod.GET`
===================================
 .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/api/user", "/api/admin").authenticated()
 - This rule applies only to GET requests for the `/api/user` and `/api/admin` endpoints.
 -  `HttpMethod.GET` → Matches only GET requests.
 - `"/api/user", "/api/admin"` → Specifies the URL patterns to match.
 - `.authenticated()` → The user must be authenticated to access these endpoints.
 - It does not check for a specific role. Any authenticated user can access these endpoints.
 
 requestMatchers() with permitAll()
 ===========================
	- .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/api/welcome")
	- .permitAll()
			This rule allows everyone to access the /api/welcome endpoint using a GET request, without authentication.
			•	HttpMethod.GET → Matches only GET requests.
			•	"/api/welcome" → Specifies the URL pattern to match.
			•	.permitAll() → Allows access to everyone, including unauthenticated users.
 
Form-Based Authentication vs HTTP Basic Authentication
==============================================
1. Form-Based Authentication
	Use form-based authentication when the application has a web UI where users log in through a login page.
	http.formLogin(withDefaults());
 
2. HTTP Basic Authentication
	Use HTTP Basic Authentication mainly when a client needs to authenticate with the server using a simple username and password sent through the HTTP Authorization header.
	http.httpBasic(withDefaults());
   
- To disable basic authentications
  http.httpBasic(basic -> basic.disable());
  
