Role Based Authorization
=======================
 - In Spring Security, we use role-based authorization to restrict access to REST APIs based on the user's role.
 - If we want to allow access to users with a single specific role, we use:

    hasRole()

 - If we want to allow access to users with multiple possible roles, we use:

    hasAnyRole()


**Example:**

	.requestMatchers("/api/admin").hasRole("ADMIN")
	Only users with the `ADMIN` role can access the API.

	.requestMatchers("/api/user").hasAnyRole("ADMIN", "USER")
	Users with either `ADMIN` or `USER` role can access the API.
	
Method Level Security 
====================================
 - Role-Based Authorization Using Method-Level Security in REST APIs
 - Instead of configuring role-based authorization in the SecurityFilterChain, we can configure authorization directly on top of REST API methods using method-level security.
 - To enable method-level security, we need to add the @EnableMethodSecurity annotation to our Spring Security configuration class.

	@Configuration
	@EnableMethodSecurity
	public class SecurityConfig {
	    // Security configuration
	}

 - After enabling method-level security, we can use annotations such as @PreAuthorize on our REST API methods.

	Example
	@GetMapping("/admin")
	@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
	public String admin() {
	    return "Admin Information";
	}

 - Here, only users with the ADMIN role can access the /admin API.

	@GetMapping("/user")
	@PreAuthorize("hasAnyRole('USER', 'ADMIN')")
	public String user() {
	    return "User Information";
	}

 - Here, users with either the USER or ADMIN role can access the /user API.

 - Key Point
	With method-level security, authorization rules are defined directly at the method level, instead of defining every authorization rule inside the SecurityFilterChain.
	

	1. @PreAuthorize
	---------------------------------
	@PreAuthorize checks authorization before the method is executed.
	It is used when we want to check whether the authenticated user has the required role or authority before allowing the method to execute.
	
	2. @PostAuthorize
	---------------------------------
	@PostAuthorize checks authorization after the method has executed.
	It is useful when the authorization decision depends on the object returned by the method.
  
  	3. @Secured
  	----------------------------------
 	@Secured is used for simple role/authority-based authorization at the method level.
 	
 	
 	
